TOPICS = [
('Introduction to Cyber Safety','Learn the basic habits that reduce avoidable digital risk.','Cyber safety means making deliberate choices that protect accounts, information, devices and other people when using digital services.'),
('Cybercrime','Understand what cybercrime is and how common offences affect people and organisations.','Cybercrime uses digital systems as a target, tool or environment for unlawful activity.'),
('Phishing','Recognise deceptive messages and verify requests before clicking, replying or signing in.','Phishing attempts to trick people into revealing information, opening harmful content or taking an unsafe action.'),
('Online Scams','Identify common scam patterns and respond without sending money or sensitive information.','Online scams use deception to obtain money, information, access or other benefits.'),
('Password Security','Build safer password habits and protect important accounts.','Unique, hard-to-guess passwords and appropriate authentication controls reduce account takeover risk.'),
('Social Media Safety','Use social platforms while controlling what you share, who can interact with you and what you trust.','Safer social media use combines privacy choices, careful sharing and healthy responses to suspicious interactions.'),
('Personal Information Protection','Understand personal information and make safer choices about collection, sharing and storage.','Personal information can create privacy and security risks when exposed, overshared or handled without care.'),
('Malware Awareness','Recognise malicious software risks and safer device habits.','Malware includes harmful software designed to disrupt, damage, spy on, or gain unauthorised access to systems.'),
('Safe Internet Browsing','Use websites and downloads more safely and evaluate links and destinations.','Safer browsing involves checking addresses, using trusted sources, keeping software updated and avoiding unnecessary downloads.'),
('Email Security','Handle email messages, attachments and account access with care.','Email is useful but can carry phishing, malicious attachments, spoofed identities and accidental data exposure.'),
('Cyberbullying','Recognise cyberbullying and respond in ways that protect safety and preserve useful evidence.','Cyberbullying involves harmful behaviour using digital communication and can affect wellbeing, privacy and participation.'),
('Two-Factor Authentication','Understand why a second authentication factor adds protection and how to use it safely.','Two-factor authentication requires two different categories of evidence to access an account.'),
]

LESSONS = {'Introduction to Cyber Safety': {'title': 'Cyber Safety Foundations: Making Safer Digital Decisions', 'objectives': 'Explain cyber safety, identify common everyday risks, and apply a practical pause–check–verify–act routine before using digital services.', 'content': "Cyber safety is the practice of using computers, phones, accounts, networks and online services in ways that reduce avoidable harm. The written project describes cyber safety as a set of practical habits that help people protect accounts, information, devices and other users. It is different from trying to predict every possible cyber attack. The goal is to build consistent decisions that remain useful across websites, social media, email, messaging and learning platforms.\n\nWhy it matters. Many incidents begin with an ordinary action: clicking a link, downloading a file, sharing a photograph, entering a password, approving a login or sending money. A safer user pauses when a request is unexpected, checks what is being asked, and verifies important instructions through a trusted route. This simple pause reduces the chance of acting under pressure.\n\nThe information involved also matters. Passwords, one-time codes, identity details, financial information, private photographs and location information should receive more protection than ordinary public information. Before sharing anything, consider who will receive it, why they need it, how it will be stored, and whether the same goal can be achieved with less information.\n\nA practical routine. First, pause when something is unusual. Second, check the sender, destination, request and consequences. Third, verify independently instead of relying only on the message itself. Fourth, act only when the request makes sense. Finally, report or seek help when something appears unsafe. This routine can be applied to phishing, scams, suspicious downloads, social-media messages and account alerts.\n\nGood cyber safety also includes responsible behaviour toward other people. Do not expose another person's private information, forward harmful content unnecessarily, or use someone else's account without permission. When a problem occurs, preserve useful evidence and use the appropriate reporting or support channel.", 'examples': 'A learner receives an unexpected message claiming that a school account will be closed in ten minutes. Instead of clicking the included sign-in link, the learner opens the normal school portal directly, checks the account and contacts the school through a trusted channel if the warning is still unclear.', 'takeaways': 'Pause before unexpected actions; check the information and requested action; verify through a trusted route; minimise sensitive sharing; protect credentials; report or seek help when something is unsafe.', 'image': 'intro.svg', 'alt': 'Shield surrounded by pause, check, verify and protect labels.'}, 'Cybercrime': {'title': 'Understanding Cybercrime and Its Impact', 'objectives': 'Define cybercrime, recognise common forms described in the project, understand how incidents can affect people and organisations, and identify safer first responses.', 'content': "Cybercrime refers to illegal activity carried out using computers, digital devices, networks or the internet. The written project identifies examples including online fraud, identity theft, unauthorised access, phishing, malware attacks, cyberbullying, financial scams and theft of personal information. These offences can affect individuals, schools, businesses and other organisations.\n\nHow cybercrime affects people. A person may lose money through fraud, lose access to an account, have private information exposed, or experience harm through abusive online behaviour. An organisation may lose data, experience service disruption, or need to investigate unauthorised access. The impact is not limited to the technical device; it can also affect privacy, finances, trust and everyday participation in digital services.\n\nRecognising the type of incident is useful. Unauthorised access suggests an account or system may have been entered without permission. Fraud involves deception for financial or other gain. Identity theft involves obtaining and using another person's information without permission. Malware uses harmful software to damage, disrupt, spy on or gain unauthorised access. Phishing and scams often rely on deception to make the victim perform an action.\n\nSafer first responses depend on the situation. Stop interacting with a suspicious requester, use legitimate account recovery or support channels, change compromised credentials through the official service, and preserve relevant evidence. If financial information has been exposed, contact the appropriate legitimate provider promptly. If a person is being threatened or harassed, seek support and use reporting mechanisms rather than escalating the situation.\n\nCyber safety education does not replace law enforcement, technical security teams or professional incident response. Its purpose is to help everyday users recognise risk early, avoid making the situation worse, and know when to report or seek assistance.", 'examples': "A learner discovers that someone has signed into an account without permission. The learner does not continue communicating with the suspected attacker. They use the service's legitimate recovery process, secure the account, review recent activity and report the incident through the appropriate support channel.", 'takeaways': 'Cybercrime can target people, accounts, devices, networks and information; recognise the type of incident; stop unsafe interaction; use legitimate recovery and reporting channels; preserve useful evidence.', 'image': 'cybercrime.svg', 'alt': 'Computer and shield illustration representing unauthorised access, fraud, data misuse and reporting.'}, 'Phishing': {'title': 'Phishing: Detecting Deceptive Messages', 'objectives': 'Identify phishing indicators, inspect senders and destinations, verify requests independently, and protect passwords and one-time authentication codes.', 'content': "Phishing is an attempt to deceive a person into revealing information, opening harmful content, transferring value, or signing in through a fraudulent route. The written project explains that a phishing message may appear to come from a trusted organisation or person and may direct the user to a fake website.\n\nCommon warning signs include unexpected account warnings, urgent deadlines, unusual requests, unfamiliar sender addresses, mismatched links, requests for passwords or one-time codes, and messages designed to create fear or excitement. A message does not need to contain spelling mistakes to be suspicious, and a polished design does not prove that it is genuine. Look at the whole context rather than relying on one clue.\n\nCheck the destination before acting. On a computer, carefully inspect the actual link destination rather than trusting only the visible words. On a phone, be cautious about shortened links and unexpected sign-in prompts. When possible, open the service using a trusted bookmark or a web address you already know rather than following the message's link.\n\nVerify important requests independently. If someone appears to be asking for money, a password, a code, a document or an urgent change, contact the person or organisation using a phone number, website or other contact method you obtained separately. Never treat a code sent to your device as something that a requester is automatically entitled to receive.\n\nIf you already clicked a suspicious link or entered credentials, do not panic. Stop further interaction, use the legitimate service to secure the account, change a compromised password, review account activity and report the message. The exact response should match what information or access may have been exposed.", 'examples': "A message says that a learner's school account will be suspended today and provides a sign-in button. The learner does not use the button. They open the normal school portal directly and verify whether any account warning actually exists.", 'takeaways': 'Unexpected does not mean malicious, but it deserves verification; inspect sender and destination; avoid message-provided sign-in links when possible; never disclose passwords or one-time codes; report suspected phishing.', 'image': 'phishing.svg', 'alt': 'Email message with warning sign, suspicious link and independent verification path.'}, 'Online Scams': {'title': 'Online Scams: Recognising Deception and Pressure', 'objectives': 'Recognise common scam patterns, identify pressure and payment red flags, verify offers independently, and respond safely when money or information may be at risk.', 'content': 'Online scams are attempts to deceive people through the internet in order to obtain money, personal information, account access or another benefit. The written project gives examples such as fake jobs, prizes, investments and products, as well as requests that persuade a person to send money or personal information.\n\nScammers often create a believable story around the request. The story may involve a prize, an urgent problem, an attractive job, an investment opportunity, a romantic relationship, a delivery, a loan, a government message or a technical support problem. The specific story changes, but the pressure pattern can be similar: act quickly, keep the matter secret, send money first, or provide sensitive information before you have verified the claim.\n\nPayment requests deserve particular care. Be suspicious of unexpected fees to unlock a prize, pressure to use unusual payment methods, requests to send money to a different account, or instructions that make it difficult to reverse a transaction. A professional-looking website, logo or social-media profile is not proof that an offer is genuine.\n\nVerify independently. Search for the organisation through a known official route, contact it using details obtained separately, and compare the claim with information you already trust. Do not use contact details supplied only by the suspicious message when those details are part of what you are trying to verify.\n\nIf a scam has already happened, act promptly. Contact the relevant bank, payment provider, platform or account provider through its legitimate support channel. Preserve messages, receipts and transaction details. Avoid sending additional money because a person claims it is needed to recover the first payment.', 'examples': "A social-media advert tells a learner that they have won a cash prize but must first pay a release fee. The learner does not pay. They check the organisation's official website and find no matching promotion, then report the suspicious advert.", 'takeaways': 'Unexpected rewards can be used to create pressure; never pay an unexpected fee just to receive a supposed prize; verify offers independently; protect payment and identity information; act promptly if money or access has been exposed.', 'image': 'scams.svg', 'alt': 'Warning triangle beside fake prize, payment and impersonation scam examples.'}, 'Password Security': {'title': 'Password Security: Building Stronger Account Protection', 'objectives': 'Explain password reuse risk, create unique credentials, use password managers appropriately, recognise stronger authentication options, and respond safely to compromised passwords.', 'content': 'A password is used to protect access to an account or system. The written project emphasises strong and unique passwords, avoiding easily guessed information, not sharing passwords, changing compromised passwords and using multi-factor authentication where available. These practices matter because one weak or reused password can create opportunities for unauthorised access.\n\nPassword reuse is especially risky. If a password is exposed at one service, an attacker may try the same email address and password combination elsewhere. Using a different password for every important account limits the damage when one service is compromised. Email accounts deserve particular protection because they can sometimes be used to reset other accounts.\n\nA strong password should be difficult to guess. Avoid names, birthdays, school names, favourite teams, simple patterns and common passwords. Long passphrases can be easier to remember while still being difficult to guess when they are unique and not based on predictable personal information.\n\nA password manager can help generate and store unique passwords so the user does not have to memorise every credential. Protect the password manager itself with a strong primary credential and appropriate additional authentication. Some services also support passkeys, which use cryptographic credentials and can reduce exposure to traditional password phishing.\n\nNever share passwords or one-time authentication codes because someone claims to be an administrator, teacher, bank employee or support agent. If a password may have been exposed, change it through the legitimate service, avoid reusing the new password elsewhere, and review account activity and recovery settings.', 'examples': 'A learner realises that the same password has been used for email, social media and another website. They create unique credentials for the important accounts, use a password manager, and enable stronger authentication on the email account.', 'takeaways': 'Use unique passwords; avoid predictable personal information; protect email and other high-value accounts carefully; consider a password manager; use passkeys or multi-factor authentication where supported; change compromised credentials promptly.', 'image': 'passwords.svg', 'alt': 'Password key, lock and separate account icons showing unique credentials.'}, 'Social Media Safety': {'title': 'Social Media Safety: Sharing With Control', 'objectives': 'Use privacy and audience settings, identify oversharing risks, protect accounts, verify suspicious contacts, and respond appropriately to harmful interactions.', 'content': "Social media platforms allow people to communicate, share information and interact with others, but the written project notes risks including fake accounts, scams, cyberbullying, privacy problems and unauthorised access. Social-media safety therefore involves both technical account protection and thoughtful decisions about what is shared.\n\nBefore posting, consider the audience and the information contained in the post. A photograph can reveal a location, school, workplace, daily routine, travel plans, contact details or other information even when the caption does not. Posts about another person can also expose their private information without their permission. Think about whether the information needs to be public at all.\n\nReview privacy and audience settings. Different platforms provide different controls, and settings can change after an update. Check who can view posts, contact the account, tag the user, comment, add the user to groups, or see location-related information. Privacy settings reduce exposure, but they should not be treated as a guarantee that content can never be copied or redistributed.\n\nProtect the account itself. Use a unique password, stronger authentication where available, and caution with unexpected login alerts. A familiar-looking profile can be compromised, so unusual requests for money, passwords, codes or private material should be verified through another trusted route.\n\nWhen an interaction becomes harmful, use blocking, muting and reporting tools as appropriate. Preserve useful evidence when necessary, seek support, and avoid retaliating by publishing another person's private information. Responsible social-media behaviour protects both the user and the people around them.", 'examples': 'A learner is tagged in a post that reveals the location of a private event. The learner removes the tag, reviews the audience settings, asks the poster to remove the location information, and avoids publishing additional details that would reveal where they will be.', 'takeaways': "Share deliberately; protect other people's information; review privacy and audience controls; secure the account; verify unusual requests; block and report harmful interactions rather than escalating them.", 'image': 'social.svg', 'alt': 'Social-media profile with privacy shield, audience controls and safe-sharing indicators.'}, 'Personal Information Protection': {'title': 'Personal Information Protection: Share With Purpose', 'objectives': 'Identify personal and sensitive information, apply data minimisation, evaluate recipients and purposes, and protect information during storage, sharing and disposal.', 'content': "Personal information is information that relates to an identifiable person. The written project gives examples such as names, phone numbers, photographs, passwords, locations and account information. Other sensitive information can include identification details, financial information, private communications and recovery information. A collection of individually harmless details can become more revealing when combined.\n\nThe first protection is data minimisation. Before providing information, ask what is actually needed for the legitimate purpose. A service may genuinely need some details, but a request for unrelated or excessive information deserves further questions. Understand who will receive the information, why it is needed, and how it will be used before sharing it.\n\nProtect information at the point of collection. Use trusted websites and applications, avoid entering sensitive information on suspicious pages, and do not send passwords or authentication codes in ordinary messages. When completing forms or uploading documents, check that the recipient and purpose are correct.\n\nProtect stored information as well. Keep devices and accounts secure, avoid leaving sensitive documents in public locations, and dispose of unnecessary records carefully. When sharing a document, consider whether every page or field is necessary. Removing unnecessary information before sharing can reduce exposure.\n\nPrivacy is also about other people. Do not publish another person's phone number, private photograph, identification information or location without an appropriate reason and permission. Once sensitive information is made public, it may be copied or redistributed beyond the original audience, so prevention is usually easier than trying to recover control later.", 'examples': 'A learner is asked to upload an identity document for a simple service. Before uploading it, the learner checks the organisation and purpose, confirms that the document is genuinely required, and avoids sharing additional information that is not necessary.', 'takeaways': "Share only what is necessary; check the recipient and purpose; protect credentials and sensitive documents; use trusted services; minimise information before sharing; respect other people's privacy.", 'image': 'privacy.svg', 'alt': 'Personal information card behind a privacy shield with minimisation and sharing controls.'}, 'Malware Awareness': {'title': 'Malware Awareness: Safer Devices and Downloads', 'objectives': 'Explain malware, recognise risky software and files, apply safer device habits, and respond appropriately when malware may be present.', 'content': 'Malware means malicious software: software designed to harm a device, disrupt activity, spy on a user, steal information or gain unauthorised access. The written project identifies malware as a common cyber threat and gives examples such as viruses, worms, spyware and ransomware. Malware can affect computers, phones and other connected devices.\n\nMalware can arrive in several ways. A user may download a malicious program, open an unsafe attachment, visit a compromised website, connect infected removable media, or install software from an untrusted source. Deceptive messages can make a harmful file appear to be an invoice, update, prize, document or useful application.\n\nSafer downloading starts with the source. Prefer official websites, reputable application stores and trusted publishers. Be cautious with pirated or cracked software, unexpected executable files, and any installer that tells you to disable security protections before it will run. A familiar file name or attractive icon is not proof that a file is safe.\n\nKeep the operating system, browser and applications updated. Updates can fix known security weaknesses. Use the security features provided by the device, review application permissions, and avoid installing software that requests access unrelated to its purpose.\n\nIf malware is suspected, avoid spreading the file or continuing risky activity. Disconnecting a compromised device from networks may be appropriate in some situations, but the correct response depends on the device and environment. Seek qualified technical support, preserve useful information about what happened, and change credentials from a known-clean device when appropriate.', 'examples': 'A website offers a cracked application and tells the learner to disable security software before installation. The learner closes the page and obtains the application from a legitimate publisher instead.', 'takeaways': 'Use trusted software sources; avoid suspicious and pirated downloads; keep systems updated; do not disable security protections to install unknown software; seek appropriate technical support when malware is suspected.', 'image': 'malware.svg', 'alt': 'Computer screen with malware bug symbol blocked by a security shield.'}, 'Safe Internet Browsing': {'title': 'Safe Internet Browsing: Links, Websites and Downloads', 'objectives': 'Check destinations and domain names, understand the limits of HTTPS, evaluate websites and downloads, and maintain safer browser and device habits.', 'content': 'Safe internet browsing means using websites, links, downloads and online services carefully. The written project advises users to check website addresses before entering sensitive information, avoid downloads from unknown sources, keep browsers and devices updated, and be careful when using public or shared computers.\n\nStart with the destination. A link can display familiar words while leading somewhere unexpected. Carefully check the actual domain name before entering passwords or payment information. Be especially cautious with misspellings, unexpected subdomains, unusual country-code domains, shortened links and destinations that do not match the organisation you expected.\n\nHTTPS is useful but has limits. HTTPS helps protect the connection between the browser and the website, but the presence of HTTPS does not prove that the website itself is legitimate. A malicious or fraudulent website can also use encrypted connections. Evaluate the domain, context, reputation and reason you arrived at the page.\n\nDownloads require another layer of caution. Prefer official publishers and reputable distribution channels. Avoid software that arrives unexpectedly or asks you to bypass security warnings. Check what an application is asking permission to access and consider whether that access makes sense for its purpose.\n\nPublic and shared computers need additional care. Avoid saving passwords, signing into sensitive accounts unnecessarily, or leaving sessions open. Log out when finished and be cautious about removable devices. Keeping software updated and using supported browsers reduces exposure to known vulnerabilities.', 'examples': "A learner searches for a free application and finds several download pages. Instead of choosing the first result, the learner finds the developer's official website, checks the publisher and downloads from the trusted source.", 'takeaways': 'Check the real destination; do not treat HTTPS as proof of legitimacy; download from trusted sources; keep browsers and devices updated; avoid saving sensitive credentials on shared computers.', 'image': 'browsing.svg', 'alt': 'Browser window showing a checked domain, secure connection and trusted download source.'}, 'Email Security': {'title': 'Email Security: Safer Messages, Links and Attachments', 'objectives': 'Recognise risky email content, verify senders and requests, handle attachments carefully, protect the email account, and prevent accidental disclosure.', 'content': "Email is a major communication tool, but it can carry phishing messages, malicious attachments, spoofed identities and accidental data exposure. A safer email habit is to slow down when a message is unexpected or asks for something sensitive.\n\nCheck the sender and context, but do not rely on the display name alone. A familiar person's account can be compromised, and a message can be designed to look like an ordinary conversation. Consider whether the request matches what you expected and whether the sender normally communicates with you in this way.\n\nTreat attachments and links carefully. An unexpected document, archive, executable file or sign-in link deserves verification before opening or using it. If the message creates urgency or asks for a password, one-time code, payment or sensitive document, verify the request through another trusted method.\n\nProtect the email account itself because it may be connected to other services through password resets. Use a unique password and stronger authentication where available. Review account alerts and recovery options and avoid sharing authentication codes with anyone who asks for them.\n\nEmail can also cause accidental disclosure. Before pressing Send, check recipients, attachments and the information contained in the message. Auto-complete can make it easy to select the wrong person, and replying to a large group can expose information to people who do not need it. Take a final moment to check the audience before sending sensitive material.", 'examples': 'A message from a familiar-looking contact asks a learner to send a document containing personal information. The learner does not reply immediately. They contact the person through another trusted channel and confirm the request before sending anything.', 'takeaways': 'Check sender and context; verify unusual requests; be cautious with attachments and links; protect the email account; never share passwords or one-time codes; check recipients before sending sensitive information.', 'image': 'email.svg', 'alt': 'Email inbox with shield, attachment warning and recipient-check symbols.'}, 'Cyberbullying': {'title': 'Cyberbullying: Safe Responses and Responsible Digital Behaviour', 'objectives': 'Recognise cyberbullying, distinguish harmful behaviour from ordinary disagreement, preserve useful evidence, use blocking and reporting tools, and seek appropriate support.', 'content': "Cyberbullying involves using digital platforms to harass, threaten, embarrass, intimidate or otherwise harm another person. The written project notes that it can occur through social media, messaging applications, online forums and other digital platforms. It can affect wellbeing, privacy, participation and a person's sense of safety.\n\nHarmful behaviour may include repeated insults, threats, humiliating posts, impersonation, exclusion, unwanted sharing of private material or coordinated harassment. Not every disagreement is cyberbullying, but a situation that creates ongoing fear, humiliation or credible threats deserves attention. The safest response focuses on protection rather than winning an argument.\n\nPreserve useful evidence when appropriate. Screenshots, dates, usernames, message links and other relevant details can help a trusted adult, school, platform or responsible organisation understand what happened. Avoid editing evidence unnecessarily and keep it somewhere safe. Do not repost harmful material just to prove that it exists if doing so would spread the harm further.\n\nUse available controls. Blocking or muting can reduce unwanted contact. Reporting tools can notify the platform about harmful behaviour. Tell a trusted person or responsible organisation, especially when the situation is persistent or affects school, work or personal safety.\n\nDo not retaliate by publishing the other person's private information, threatening them, or encouraging others to attack them. If there is a credible threat of physical harm or an immediate safety concern, seek urgent help through an appropriate trusted person or local emergency/support channel.", 'examples': 'A learner receives repeated abusive messages in a group chat. Instead of responding with insults, the learner preserves relevant messages, blocks the account, reports the behaviour and tells a trusted adult or responsible staff member.', 'takeaways': 'Prioritise safety over retaliation; preserve useful evidence; block and report harmful behaviour; seek support; protect privacy; seek urgent help for credible threats of physical harm.', 'image': 'cyberbullying.svg', 'alt': 'Chat messages with stop symbol, evidence capture and support icons.'}, 'Two-Factor Authentication': {'title': 'Two-Factor Authentication: Adding a Second Layer', 'objectives': 'Explain two-factor authentication, distinguish authentication factor categories, protect one-time codes and devices, and use stronger authentication safely.', 'content': 'Two-factor authentication (2FA) is a security method that requires two different categories of evidence before access is granted. The written project explains that, after a password, a user may be asked for a verification code sent to a phone or generated by an authentication application. The purpose is to add another layer of protection if the password becomes known to another person.\n\nAuthentication factors are commonly grouped into categories such as something you know, something you have and something you are. A password is something you know. A code generated by an authenticator application or a security key is associated with something you have. Biometric verification is an example of something you are. Two passwords do not normally count as two different factors because they are the same category.\n\n2FA reduces risk but does not make an account invulnerable. An attacker may still try to steal a second factor through social engineering, malicious prompts or other methods. This is why a one-time code should never be disclosed to a person who asks for it unexpectedly. If a login prompt appears that you did not initiate, do not approve it simply because the screen looks official.\n\nProtect recovery methods too. Keep authentication devices secure, store recovery codes safely, and understand how the service lets you regain access if a device is lost. Where supported, stronger phishing-resistant methods such as passkeys or security keys can provide additional protection.\n\nUse 2FA on important accounts where it is available, especially accounts containing valuable personal information or accounts that can reset other services. If you suspect that a code or authentication device has been compromised, use the legitimate account-security process promptly.', 'examples': "A caller claims to be from a service provider and says there is a problem with a learner's account. The caller asks for the one-time code that has just arrived on the learner's phone. The learner refuses to provide the code and contacts the provider using its official support route instead.", 'takeaways': 'Use 2FA where available; understand that the factors should be different categories; never share one-time codes; protect authentication devices and recovery methods; consider phishing-resistant options when supported.', 'image': '2fa.svg', 'alt': 'Login screen showing a password plus a separate authentication-code factor.'}}

QUESTION_BANK = {
'Introduction to Cyber Safety': [('A message says an account will close today unless you use its link. What should you do first?',['Use the link immediately','Open the service through a trusted route and check the account','Reply with your password','Forward it to everyone you know'],1,'Using an independently trusted route avoids relying on the message itself.'),('Which habit best describes a basic cyber-safety approach?',['Act quickly when a message creates pressure','Pause and verify unexpected requests','Reuse one password everywhere','Share sensitive information to prove identity'],1,'Pausing and verifying reduces avoidable mistakes.'),('Why should you consider what information a request involves?',['All information has the same risk','Sensitive information can create greater harm if exposed','It makes messages shorter','It guarantees the sender is genuine'],1,'The sensitivity and potential impact of information affect the risk of an action.'),('What is a safer response when you suspect an incident?',['Continue the risky action','Stop further exposure and use a trusted reporting or recovery route','Delete every record immediately','Contact the suspicious sender for instructions'],1,'Stopping exposure and using legitimate recovery/reporting processes are safer steps.')],
'Cybercrime': [('Which statement best describes cybercrime?',['Any mistake made online','Unlawful activity involving digital systems, accounts, networks or data','Only computer viruses','Only attacks against large companies'],1,'Cybercrime covers a range of unlawful digital activity.'),('If an account was accessed without permission, what should the owner use?',['The attacker’s contact details','The legitimate account recovery process','A public post containing the password','A random download'],1,'The legitimate provider can secure the account and guide recovery.'),('Why is identifying the type of incident useful?',['It determines the safest response and reporting route','It makes the incident harmless','It guarantees the attacker is known','It removes the need for evidence'],0,'Understanding what happened helps determine an appropriate response.'),('Which could be a cybercrime example?',['Choosing a strong password','Unauthorised access to another person’s account','Updating software','Using privacy settings'],1,'Unauthorised access is an example of unlawful digital activity.')],
'Phishing': [('Which is a common phishing warning sign?',['An expected receipt from a service you just used','An unexpected urgent request for credentials','A bookmark you created yourself','A document you saved locally'],1,'Unexpected urgency and credential requests are common phishing cues.'),('How should you verify an unusual sign-in request?',['Use the link in the message','Use a trusted bookmark or independently known address','Reply with your one-time code','Ask the sender to send your password'],1,'Independent verification avoids relying on the suspicious message.'),('Why is one clue not enough to prove a message is malicious?',['Messages never contain clues','Legitimate messages can also contain some warning-like features','Phishing has no patterns','Every sender is trustworthy'],1,'The overall context and multiple signals matter.'),('What should you never give a requester through a suspicious message?',['A public website address','A password or one-time authentication code','The name of a public service','A general greeting'],1,'Passwords and authentication codes should not be disclosed to requesters.')],
'Online Scams': [('Which pattern is a common scam warning sign?',['A guaranteed reward requiring an unexpected payment first','A normal purchase you initiated','A receipt for a purchase you made','A public service announcement'],0,'Advance-fee and guaranteed-reward claims are common scam patterns.'),('What should you do with an unexpected prize claim?',['Pay the release fee','Verify it independently before sharing information or money','Send a copy of your ID immediately','Reply with your bank password'],1,'Independent verification is safer than trusting the claim.'),('Why do scammers use urgency?',['To give you more time to investigate','To pressure you into acting before checking the claim','To improve account security','To make the message easier to read'],1,'Pressure can reduce the chance that a person pauses and verifies.'),('If money has already been sent to a suspected scammer, what is a useful next step?',['Ignore it','Contact the relevant legitimate payment provider promptly','Send more money to recover it','Delete all records'],1,'Prompt contact with the legitimate provider may allow protective or recovery steps.')],
'Password Security': [('Why is password reuse risky?',['One exposed password may be tried on other accounts','It makes passwords longer','It automatically enables 2FA','It prevents phishing'],0,'Unique passwords reduce the chain of risk from one exposed credential.'),('Which password practice is safer?',['Use your birthday','Use unique, hard-to-guess credentials','Use the same short password everywhere','Post your password in a private group'],1,'Unique and hard-to-guess credentials are safer.'),('What can a password manager help with?',['Generating and storing unique passwords','Disabling security updates','Sharing one password publicly','Removing the need for all authentication'],0,'Password managers reduce the need to reuse or memorise many passwords.'),('What is a passkey?',['A public social-media password','A cryptographic authentication credential supported by some services','A recovery question','A type of scam'],1,'Passkeys use cryptographic credentials rather than traditional reusable passwords.')],
'Social Media Safety': [('What should you consider before posting personal details?',['Whether the information could reveal sensitive details or another person’s privacy','Only how many likes it may receive','Whether strangers can copy it instantly','Nothing'],0,'Consider the audience, sensitivity and impact of the information.'),('What should you do if a familiar-looking account makes an unusual money request?',['Send the money quickly','Verify through another trusted contact method','Post the request publicly with the person’s private details','Send your password'],1,'A familiar account can be compromised, so unusual requests should be independently verified.'),('Which action can reduce unwanted social-media exposure?',['Reviewing privacy and audience settings','Posting more personal information','Accepting every follower','Sharing location publicly by default'],0,'Privacy and audience settings can limit exposure.'),('What should you avoid when responding to harmful online behaviour?',['Using reporting tools','Seeking support','Retaliating by exposing private information','Blocking an account'],2,'Retaliation can escalate harm and create another privacy violation.')],
'Personal Information Protection': [('Which is an example of personal information?',['A random weather fact','An account credential','A public mathematical formula','A generic software logo'],1,'Account credentials are information connected to a person and can enable access.'),('What does data minimisation mean in practice?',['Provide every requested detail','Provide only information that is necessary for the legitimate purpose','Publish all information publicly','Never provide any information'],1,'Minimisation reduces unnecessary exposure.'),('Why can several small pieces of information matter together?',['They can combine to reveal more about a person','They always become public automatically','They stop being personal','They cannot be copied'],0,'Combined information can create a more complete and sensitive profile.'),('Before sharing a document containing personal information, what should you check?',['The recipient and purpose','Only the file name','Whether the document looks attractive','How quickly you can send it'],0,'Checking the recipient and purpose helps prevent accidental disclosure.')],
'Malware Awareness': [('What is malware?',['Software designed to perform harmful or unauthorised actions','Any software update','A secure password','A privacy setting'],0,'Malware is harmful or unauthorised software.'),('Which download is a strong warning sign?',['Software from a reputable official source','A cracked application that asks you to disable security protections','A routine browser update','A document you created yourself'],1,'Cracked software and instructions to disable protections are significant warning signs.'),('What helps reduce exposure to known software vulnerabilities?',['Keeping systems and applications updated','Disabling updates','Installing unknown programs','Ignoring security alerts'],0,'Updates often address known vulnerabilities.'),('If malware is suspected, what is a safer response?',['Keep spreading the file','Avoid further exposure and seek appropriate technical support','Disable every security control permanently','Send the suspected file to friends'],1,'Limiting spread and seeking qualified support is safer.')],
'Safe Internet Browsing': [('What does HTTPS prove?',['That a site is always legitimate','That the connection is encrypted in transit','That the publisher is trustworthy','That a download is safe'],1,'HTTPS protects the connection but does not by itself prove site legitimacy.'),('What should you check before downloading software?',['The publisher and source','Only the file colour','Whether the filename is funny','How many pop-ups appear'],0,'Trusted publishers and official sources reduce download risk.'),('Why check the actual domain name?',['A deceptive site can use a familiar-looking page','Domains never matter','It changes your password automatically','It guarantees the site is free'],0,'The destination domain is an important part of evaluating a link.'),('Which habit supports safer browsing?',['Keeping the browser and operating system updated','Installing every browser extension','Ignoring update prompts forever','Using unknown download sites'],0,'Keeping software updated helps reduce exposure to known vulnerabilities.')],
'Email Security': [('What should you do with an unexpected attachment?',['Open it immediately','Check the sender and context and verify when necessary','Forward it to everyone','Disable security protections'],1,'Unexpected attachments should be treated cautiously.'),('Why can a familiar sender still be risky?',['Their account could have been compromised','Familiar accounts cannot send files','Email addresses never change','It guarantees the message is fake'],0,'Compromised accounts can be used to send harmful messages.'),('What should you check before sending sensitive information by email?',['The recipient and whether the sharing is appropriate','Only the subject line','How many emojis are included','Whether the message is long'],0,'Recipient checking helps prevent accidental disclosure.'),('Which control can help protect an email account?',['A unique password and stronger authentication where available','Sharing the password with colleagues','Using one password everywhere','Disabling account alerts'],0,'Unique credentials and stronger authentication improve account protection.')],
'Cyberbullying': [('What is cyberbullying?',['Harmful behaviour carried out through digital communication','Any disagreement online','A software update','A privacy setting'],0,'Cyberbullying involves harmful behaviour using digital communication.'),('Why preserve relevant messages or screenshots?',['They can provide useful evidence for reporting','They make the bully stop automatically','They should be posted publicly','They expose the victim’s password'],0,'Records can help trusted adults, schools or platforms understand what happened.'),('Which response avoids escalation?',['Retaliate with private information','Block/report and seek support','Threaten the person publicly','Share their personal details'],1,'Blocking, reporting and seeking support prioritise safety without retaliation.'),('When should urgent help be sought?',['When there is a credible threat of physical harm','Only after deleting all evidence','Never','Only when a post gets many likes'],0,'Credible threats of physical harm warrant prompt support through appropriate channels.')],
'Two-Factor Authentication': [('What does 2FA require?',['Two different categories of authentication evidence','Two passwords of the same type','A username only','A public profile'],0,'Two-factor authentication uses two different factor categories.'),('Why does 2FA help after a password is stolen?',['The attacker may still lack the second factor','It makes passwords unnecessary in every system','It prevents all cybercrime','It publishes the password'],0,'A stolen password alone may not be enough to complete authentication.'),('What should you do if someone asks for your one-time code?',['Share it if they sound official','Do not share it and contact the provider through a trusted route if needed','Post it publicly','Send it to a friend'],1,'One-time codes are authentication secrets and should not be disclosed to requesters.'),('Which is an example of two different factors?',['Password plus authenticator-app code','Password plus another password','Username plus email address','Two security questions'],0,'A password is something you know; an authenticator code is typically something you have or access through a device.')],
}

def slug(s): return re.sub(r'[^a-z0-9]+','-',s.lower()).strip('-')

def seed_database():
    for pos, (title, desc, intro) in enumerate(TOPICS, 1):
        topic = Topic.query.filter_by(title=title).first()
        if not topic:
            topic = Topic(title=title, slug=slug(title), description=desc, position=pos); db.session.add(topic); db.session.flush()
        else:
            topic.description, topic.position = desc, pos
        data = LESSONS[title]
        # Populate all required lesson fields before the first INSERT/flush.
        # This prevents SQLite from rejecting a newly created lesson with a NULL title.
        lesson = topic.lessons[0] if topic.lessons else Lesson(topic_id=topic.id, position=1)
        lesson.title = data['title']
        lesson.objectives = data['objectives']
        lesson.content = data['content']
        lesson.examples = data['examples']
        lesson.takeaways = data['takeaways']
        lesson.image_path = f"images/lessons/{data['image']}"
        lesson.image_alt = data['alt']
        lesson.position = 1
        if not lesson.id:
            db.session.add(lesson)
        db.session.flush()
        QuizQuestion.query.filter_by(lesson_id=lesson.id).delete(synchronize_session=False)
        for q, options, correct, explanation in QUESTION_BANK[title]:
            db.session.add(QuizQuestion(lesson_id=lesson.id, question=q, options=json.dumps(options), correct_index=correct, explanation=explanation))
    # Do not delete or rewrite administrator-created topics here. This function
    # is used only to initialize a brand-new database; production startup never
    # calls it once topics already exist.
    admin_email=os.getenv('ADMIN_EMAIL','admin@cybersafe.local').strip().lower(); admin_password=os.getenv('ADMIN_PASSWORD','Admin@12345')
    admin=User.query.filter_by(email=admin_email).first()
    if not admin:
        admin=User(full_name='CS CyberSafe Administrator', email=admin_email, role='admin', is_active=True); admin.set_password(admin_password); db.session.add(admin)
    else: admin.role='admin'; admin.is_active=True
    demo_email=os.getenv('DEMO_STUDENT_EMAIL','').strip().lower(); demo_password=os.getenv('DEMO_STUDENT_PASSWORD','')
    if demo_email and demo_password:
        demo=User.query.filter_by(email=demo_email).first()
        if not demo:
            demo=User(full_name='Demo Student', email=demo_email, role='student', is_active=True); demo.set_password(demo_password); db.session.add(demo)
    db.session.commit()

# The project source defines the 12 cyber-safety subject areas; the following
# additions make each lesson more substantial without changing that curriculum.
EXPANSIONS = {
'Introduction to Cyber Safety': """A useful way to think about cyber safety is to separate the information, the action and the consequence. Before an online action, ask what information is being requested, what permission is being granted, and what could happen if the request is wrong. This makes cyber safety practical rather than abstract.

Everyday protection also depends on habits. Keep devices updated, use screen locks, protect account credentials, review privacy settings and avoid installing software from unknown sources. Small habits are valuable because they are repeated across many digital situations.

When an incident happens, avoid panic. Stop the unsafe action, disconnect from the suspicious interaction where appropriate, preserve useful evidence and use a trusted support route. Asking for help early is a responsible cyber-safety behaviour, not a failure.

The best-fit illustration for this topic shows a learner receiving an unexpected account alert and choosing to pause, open the service directly and verify the request. The example demonstrates the central principle of making a deliberate decision instead of reacting to pressure.""",
'Cybercrime': """Cybercrime can involve individuals, organised groups or other actors using digital technology to commit unlawful acts. The same incident may contain several elements: a stolen password can lead to unauthorised access, which can then lead to information theft or fraud. Recognising these connections helps a learner understand why one weak point can have wider consequences.

A safe first response is evidence-aware. Do not delete every message immediately if the information may help explain what happened. Record dates, account alerts, usernames, transaction references or other relevant details, while avoiding unnecessary public sharing of sensitive material.

Recovery should use legitimate channels. If an account is compromised, use the service's official recovery process rather than a contact supplied by the suspected attacker. If money or identity information is involved, contact the appropriate legitimate institution promptly and follow its incident process.

The illustration presents an unauthorised-access situation and the safer response: secure the affected account, preserve useful evidence and report through a legitimate route. It shows that cyber safety includes knowing what to do after a problem, not only preventing one.""",
'Phishing': """Phishing often succeeds because the message looks believable enough to trigger a quick reaction. The message may use a familiar logo, an urgent deadline or language that sounds like an ordinary service notification. The safest approach is to inspect several clues together instead of deciding based on appearance alone.

Look at the real sender address and the destination of links. A displayed name can be misleading, and a familiar-looking domain can contain an extra word or altered spelling. Be especially careful when a message asks for a password, one-time code, payment, identity document or other sensitive information.

Independent verification is the strongest habit. If a bank, school, employer or online service appears to contact you, open its normal website or application yourself and check for the alert there. You can also contact the organisation using a trusted number or address that you already know.

The illustration shows a realistic account-verification message with a suspicious destination. The safer response is to ignore the embedded sign-in route and open the genuine service independently. This is the difference between reacting to a message and verifying a request.""",
'Online Scams': """Scams are built around deception, and many are designed to create a strong emotion before the target has time to think. Promises of prizes, jobs, investments, refunds or urgent assistance can be used to make an offer appear valuable or time-sensitive.

A useful check is to ask what the other person wants and why. Requests for advance fees, gift cards, cryptocurrency transfers, banking credentials, identity documents or remote access deserve careful verification. A genuine opportunity should not require you to ignore sensible checks simply because someone says the offer will disappear soon.

Do not use contact information supplied only by the suspicious message to verify the claim. Search for the organisation independently, use its official contact channel and compare the information. If you already sent money or sensitive information, act quickly through the legitimate provider involved and preserve transaction details.

The illustration shows a fake prize message demanding an immediate payment. It demonstrates a common scam pattern: a valuable promise followed by pressure for money or information. The safer decision is to stop, verify the offer and refuse unnecessary payment or disclosure.""",
'Password Security': """Password security is not only about making one password complicated. It is about preventing one compromised credential from opening several accounts. Reusing the same password across email, social media and financial services creates a chain of risk because an attacker can try stolen credentials elsewhere.

Long, unique passwords or passphrases are preferable to predictable personal information. A password manager can help create and store different credentials where appropriate. Learners should also understand that passwords should not be shared through messages, notes or informal requests.

Account recovery deserves attention too. Keep recovery email addresses and phone numbers current, protect recovery codes and review security alerts. If a password is exposed, change it through the legitimate service and consider other accounts where the same credential was reused.

The illustration presents a simple account-security checklist showing unique credentials, appropriate password-management support and stronger authentication. It turns the topic into a practical routine rather than a rule about symbols and numbers alone.""",
'Social Media Safety': """Social media safety begins with understanding that posts can travel beyond the intended audience. Screenshots, forwarding and changes to platform settings can make information difficult to control once it has been shared. Think carefully before posting photographs, school details, travel plans, location information or other sensitive material.

Review privacy and audience controls periodically. Also review who can send messages, tag you, add you to groups or see older posts. These settings do not replace good judgement, but they can reduce unnecessary exposure.

Be cautious with direct messages as well. A profile that looks familiar can be fake or compromised. Do not assume that a request for money, a link or a private conversation is safe simply because it came through a social platform.

The illustration shows a social-media post with an audience-control decision. The example emphasises three practical questions: who can see this, what information does it reveal, and do I trust the person or request?""",
'Personal Information Protection': """Personal information includes details that identify, describe or can be connected to a person. Names, phone numbers, photographs, locations, account credentials and identification information can become more sensitive when combined. The project specifically highlights the importance of controlling how personal information is shared and used.

Use data minimisation as a practical habit. If a form asks for several details, consider which fields are genuinely necessary for the stated purpose. Avoid providing additional information simply because it is requested. Before sharing a document, check whether it contains hidden or unrelated personal details.

Consider the recipient, purpose and storage. Sending a document to the wrong person can create an immediate disclosure, while publishing information publicly can make later control difficult. When a service requests sensitive data, confirm that you are using the legitimate service and understand why the information is needed.

The illustration shows an online form requesting personal data. The safe response is to check the purpose, provide only necessary information and confirm the legitimate recipient. This makes privacy a concrete decision at the point of sharing.""",
'Malware Awareness': """Malware is malicious software, including examples such as viruses, worms, spyware and ransomware. It may arrive through downloads, attachments, compromised websites or other channels. The important lesson for everyday users is that an apparently useful file or application can contain a hidden risk.

Be especially cautious with cracked software, unofficial installers and files that ask you to disable security protections. A request to turn off a security tool in order to install an unknown program is a strong reason to stop and verify the source.

Keep the operating system, browser and applications updated. Use reputable security software where available and avoid opening unexpected files simply to see what they contain. If you suspect an infection, avoid spreading the file or continuing risky activity and seek appropriate technical support.

The illustration shows a tempting free application that is actually an unsafe executable. It demonstrates the decision point where a learner should stop the download and choose a trusted source instead.""",
'Safe Internet Browsing': """Safe browsing requires attention to the destination, not only the appearance of a webpage. A fraudulent website can copy familiar colours, logos and layouts. Before entering sensitive information, check the address carefully and consider whether you reached the site through a trusted route.

HTTPS is useful because it protects data in transit, but it does not prove that the site operator is trustworthy. A malicious or deceptive website can also use encrypted connections. The domain, context and purpose still need to be considered.

Downloads require similar care. Prefer official publishers and trusted stores or repositories. Be suspicious of unexpected pop-ups, forced downloads, browser warnings that demand immediate action, and instructions to install unknown extensions or software.

The illustration focuses on inspecting a website address before signing in or downloading. It reinforces the idea that a safe browser habit is a sequence of checks rather than a single visual sign of trust.""",
'Email Security': """Email security starts with context. A message from a familiar name is not automatically safe because accounts can be compromised and display names can be copied. Think about whether the message was expected, whether the request matches the sender's normal behaviour and whether it asks for something unusually sensitive.

Attachments deserve particular care. Unexpected executable files, archives or documents with unusual instructions should be verified before opening. Links that lead to sign-in pages should be checked carefully, especially when the message creates urgency.

Protect the email account itself because it can be used to reset other accounts. Use a unique password, stronger authentication where available and reliable recovery options. Review security alerts so that unusual access can be noticed early.

Email can also create accidental disclosure. Before sending sensitive information, check the recipient, copied recipients and attachments. Auto-complete can make it easy to choose the wrong contact, so a final review before pressing Send is a useful safety habit.

The illustration shows an unexpected attachment and the safer sequence of checking the sender, verifying the request and checking recipients before sending information.""",
'Cyberbullying': """Cyberbullying can happen through social media, messaging applications, online communities and other digital platforms. Harmful behaviour may include repeated insults, threats, humiliation, impersonation, unwanted sharing of private material or coordinated harassment. The important distinction is that the response should protect the affected person rather than escalate the conflict.

Preserve useful evidence where appropriate. Keep relevant screenshots, dates, usernames, message links and other details that can help a trusted adult, school, platform or responsible organisation understand what occurred. Avoid reposting harmful material widely simply to prove that it exists.

Use platform controls such as blocking, muting and reporting. Seek support from a trusted person when the behaviour continues or affects school, work or personal safety. If there is a credible threat of physical harm, seek urgent assistance through an appropriate support or emergency channel.

The illustration presents repeated harmful messages and the three-part safer response: preserve evidence, block/report and seek support. It deliberately avoids retaliation because publishing another person's private information can create additional harm.""",
'Two-Factor Authentication': """Two-factor authentication adds another layer because it uses two different categories of evidence. A password is something you know, while an authenticator code, security key or other device-based method is generally associated with something you have. Biometric verification is an example of something you are.

The second factor should be protected like a password. Never give an unexpected caller or message a one-time code simply because the person claims to be from a trusted organisation. If a login prompt appears that you did not initiate, do not approve it automatically.

Recovery matters too. Store recovery codes safely, protect the device used for authentication and understand the service's legitimate recovery process. Where supported, stronger phishing-resistant methods such as passkeys or security keys can provide additional protection.

The illustration shows a sign-in request followed by a separate verification code. It demonstrates the correct decision point: approve only a login you initiated and never disclose the code to someone who asks for it.""",
}

for _title, _extra in EXPANSIONS.items():
    if _title in LESSONS:
        LESSONS[_title]['content'] = LESSONS[_title]['content'].rstrip() + '\n\n' + _extra

